EDITOR HELP
===========

A vi-like text editor for the 6502 platform.

Usage: editor <filename>

If the file does not exist, starts with an empty buffer.
If no filename is given, starts with [No Name].
Maximum file size: ~40KB. Files exceeding this are truncated and
opened read-only.
Maximum lines: 1023.


MODES
-----

The editor has three modes:

  NORMAL   Default mode for navigation and commands.
  INSERT   Text entry mode. Entered via i, a, A, o, O, s, S, C, cc, cw, cb.
  COMMAND  Colon command-line mode. Entered via :.


NORMAL MODE - MOVEMENT
----------------------

  h, Left        Move cursor left (accepts count)
  l, Right       Move cursor right (accepts count)
  j, Down        Move cursor down (accepts count)
  k, Up          Move cursor up (accepts count)
  0, Home        Move to beginning of line
  $, End         Move to end of line (last character)
  ^              Move to first non-blank character on line
  w              Move to start of next word (accepts count)
  b              Move to start of previous word (accepts count)
  e              Move to end of current/next word (accepts count)
  G              Go to last line; [N]G goes to line N
  gg             Go to first line
  Ctrl-F, PgDn   Scroll forward one screen
  Ctrl-B, PgUp   Scroll backward one screen

  Word motions cross line boundaries. Words are classified as:
  word characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, _), punctuation, or whitespace.
  A word boundary is a transition between classes.


NORMAL MODE - EDITING
---------------------

All editing commands are blocked in read-only mode.

  i              Enter insert mode at cursor
  a              Enter insert mode after cursor
  A              Enter insert mode at end of line
  o              Open new line below, enter insert mode
  O              Open new line above, enter insert mode
  x, Delete      Delete character at cursor (accepts count)
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  D              Delete from cursor to end of line
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  dd             Delete current line (accepts count: [N]dd)
                 Yanks deleted lines first (line yank)
  dw             Delete from cursor to next word boundary (accepts count)
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  db             Delete backward to word start (accepts count)
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  cc             Delete current line, open blank line, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count: [N]cc). Yanks deleted lines (line yank)
  cw             Change word: delete to end of word, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  cb             Change backward: delete to word start, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  C              Delete from cursor to end of line, enter insert mode
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  s              Delete character(s) at cursor, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  S              Delete current line, open blank line, enter insert mode
                 Same as cc (alias)
  r              Replace character at cursor (accepts count: [N]r)
                 Waits for next keypress, replaces N characters
  ~              Toggle case of character at cursor (accepts count)
                 Advances cursor after each toggle
  J              Join current line with next line (accepts count: [N]J)
                 Replaces newline with a space. With count N, joins N lines
  >>             Indent current line by 2 spaces (accepts count: [N]>>)
  <<             Unindent current line by up to 2 spaces (accepts count)
  yy             Yank (copy) current line (accepts count: [N]yy)
                 Does not modify the file
  p              Paste after/below cursor (accepts count: [N]p)
                 Line yank: pastes lines below current line
                 Char yank: inserts after cursor position
  P              Paste before/above cursor (accepts count: [N]P)
                 Line yank: pastes lines above current line
                 Char yank: inserts before cursor position


NORMAL MODE - SEARCH
--------------------

  /              Enter forward search prompt. Type a pattern and press
                 Enter. Empty pattern reuses the previous search.
                 ESC cancels. Search is literal (no regex).
  ?              Enter backward search prompt. Same behavior as /
                 but searches backward through the file.
  n              Repeat last search in the same direction (wraps around)
  N              Repeat last search in the opposite direction (wraps around)

  If the pattern is not found, "Pattern not found" is displayed.


NORMAL MODE - MARKS
-------------------

  ma             Set mark 'a' at current line (a-z available)
  'a             Jump to mark 'a' (cursor goes to column 0)

  Marks are automatically adjusted when lines are inserted or
  deleted. Marks within a deleted range are cleared.


NORMAL MODE - COUNT PREFIX
--------------------------

  Type a number (1-9 to start, then 0-9) before a command to
  repeat it. Examples:

    5j             Move down 5 lines
    3dd            Delete 3 lines
    10x            Delete 10 characters
    2p             Paste 2 times
    4w             Move forward 4 words
    3>>            Indent 3 lines
    5~             Toggle case of 5 characters
    3J             Join 3 lines (current + 2 below)

  Counts are capped at 1000. ESC clears an active count.
  0 alone moves to line start; it does not start a count.


NORMAL MODE - OTHER
-------------------

  :              Enter command-line mode
  ESC            Clear count prefix and/or pending two-key command


INSERT MODE
-----------

  Printable chars   Insert at cursor position
  Enter             Split line (insert newline)
  Backspace         Delete character before cursor; at column 0,
                    joins with the previous line
  Delete            Delete character at cursor; at end of line,
                    joins with the next line
  ESC               Return to normal mode (cursor moves back one)

  Arrow keys, Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B all work
  for navigation within insert mode. The cursor can be positioned
  one past the last character (for appending).


COMMAND-LINE MODE
-----------------

  :w             Save file
  :q             Quit (warns if file is modified)
  :wq            Save and quit
  :q!            Quit without saving
  :[N]           Go to line N (1-based)
  :marks         Display all set marks with line numbers

  Range commands:

  :[start],[end]d    Delete lines in range (yanks first)
  :[start],[end]y    Yank (copy) lines in range

  Range positions can be:
    N              Line number (1-based)
    'a             Mark reference
    .              Current line

  Examples:
    :1,5d          Delete lines 1 through 5
    :'a,'bd        Delete from mark a to mark b
    :.,'ay         Yank from current line to mark a
    :3,3d          Delete just line 3

  If start > end, the range is automatically swapped.

  ESC cancels command input. Backspace edits the command;
  backspacing past the start cancels.


YANK BUFFER
-----------

  The yank buffer (4KB) holds either complete lines or character
  data, depending on the last yank/delete operation:

  Line yank (from dd, yy, cc, range commands):
    p/P paste complete lines above or below.

  Character yank (from x, D, dw, db, cw, cb, s, C):
    p inserts after cursor (or at line start if empty line).
    P inserts before cursor.

  Each new yank/delete clears the previous yank buffer contents.


STATUS BAR
----------

  The bottom line shows:
    filename  [RO]  [+]  mode  count/pending  line,col  total

  [RO]  = read-only (file was truncated)
  [+]   = file has been modified since last save


DISPLAY
-------

  Long lines wrap across multiple screen rows.
  Lines past end of file are shown as ~ (tilde).
  Non-ASCII bytes (high bit set) are shown as ? in reverse video.
  Control characters in file content are shown as spaces.
